from __future__ import annotations

NAME = "The Stoic Night"
AMBIENCE = "Singing Bowl"

# A soothing sleep-lecture about gentle Stoic night-counsel in the spirit of Marcus Aurelius -- letting go of what is not yours, the view from above, the calm inner citadel.
SECTIONS = [['You have nowhere to be right now.', 'The day is behind you, and the night is here.', 'Let your body grow heavy against the bed beneath you.', 'There is nothing left to do, and nothing left to fix.', 'Feel the weight of your head, soft and still upon the pillow.', 'Let your shoulders drop, a little lower, a little softer.', 'Breathe in slowly, and let the breath go without effort.', 'The room is quiet, and the quiet is kind.', 'Outside, the light has faded, and the world is settling too.', 'Let your hands rest, open and easy, wherever they lie.', 'There is nowhere else you need to be than here.', 'Just rest, and let the night draw gently in around you.'], ['Long ago, an old emperor lay down at night much as you do now.', 'He kept a small book by lamplight, writing gentle notes to himself.', 'He was not writing for anyone else, only to soothe his own tired mind.', 'And the first thing he reminded himself was simple and kind.', 'Some things, he wrote, are within your care, and many more are not.', 'What you think, and how you meet the day, these are yours.', 'But the weather, and the world, and the minds of others are not.', 'And so there is no use in clutching at what was never yours to hold.', 'You can loosen your grip now, gently, like setting down a heavy bag.', 'Let the things you cannot change drift softly out of your hands.', 'Peace, he found, lives in this quiet letting go.', 'Breathe out, and let a little of the day go with it.'], ['The old emperor told himself a comforting thing each night.', 'Do your own small part well, he wrote, and leave the rest to the world.', 'You are not asked to carry everything, only your own gentle share.', 'When you have done what is yours to do, the day is enough.', 'The rest belongs to time, and to forces far larger than you.', 'So you need not lie awake turning over what is out of your hands.', 'You did your part, kindly and as well as you could.', 'And now the world will turn as it turns, with or without your worry.', 'There is a deep rest in trusting the rest to the wide world.', 'Lay down the sense that it all depends on you, for it does not.', 'Your share for today is finished, and finished is enough.', 'Let that thought settle over you like a soft and quiet blanket.'], ['The Stoics taught gently of something they called the view from above.', 'Imagine, for a moment, rising softly into the calm night sky.', 'See yourself small and safe below, a single resting figure.', 'See your house, and your street, growing smaller and softer.', 'See the quiet town, and the dark fields, and the rivers far below.', 'From up here, under the vast and gentle sky, your worries shrink.', 'The things that loomed so large grow small as distant specks.', 'And they are still there, but they are gentle now, and far away.', 'Under so wide a sky, there is room for everything to be calm.', 'You are a small and peaceful part of a vast and quiet whole.', 'Let yourself float there a while, held by the great soft dark.', 'Then drift slowly down again, lighter than you rose.'], ['Each day, the old emperor wrote, is complete in itself.', 'When the sun goes down, the day has given all it had to give.', 'It asks nothing more of you, and you owe it nothing more.', 'Tomorrow is not here yet, and tomorrow can wait until it comes.', 'Tonight asks only that you rest, and nothing more than that.', 'You do not need to solve the morning while you lie here now.', 'Whatever waits ahead will be met when it arrives, not before.', 'For now there is only this night, and this breath, and this stillness.', 'The day is whole, and gathered up, and gently set aside.', 'Let it close softly behind you, like a door easing shut.', 'There is nothing unfinished that this night needs you to finish.', 'This night asks only that you let yourself sleep.'], ['Sometimes others are troubled, and their storms are loud.', 'But their storms, the Stoics taught, are theirs and not yours.', 'You can wish them well, kindly and sincerely, and still rest.', 'Their worry does not need to become your worry tonight.', 'You can hold them softly in your thoughts and still let go.', 'Nature too is always changing, always flowing on, the old emperor wrote.', 'The seasons turn, and the rivers move, and nothing stays fixed for long.', 'And this is not to be feared, for it is simply the way of things.', 'Change carried you here, gently, and change will carry you onward.', 'There is nothing to brace against, only a soft current to float upon.', 'Let the world flow as it flows, and let yourself flow with it.', 'Wish all things well, and let them be, and rest.'], ['And so you come back, as you always can, to the quiet place within.', 'The old emperor called it a calm citadel, a still room inside you.', 'No storm outside can enter there, for it is yours and always open.', 'You can return to it any time, simply by growing still.', 'You are there now, resting in its deep and quiet peace.', 'Lay down, one by one, the things that were never yours to carry.', 'Let them rest outside the door, soft and harmless in the dark.', 'Your part is done, and the day is whole, and the sky is wide and calm.', 'Breathe in slowly, and let it go, slower still.', 'Your thoughts grow soft and far, like specks beneath a gentle sky.', 'There is nothing left to hold, and nothing left to do but rest.', 'Let yourself sink down into the warm and quiet dark, and sleep.']]
